60,3,1,talk,Minako,,Option B aligns with the data. It tracks distance for fair cost-splitting and removes the favour-asking friction Benjamin identified.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""name_label"",""name"":""Minako"",""self"":true,""raw"":""Minako: Option B aligns with the data. It tracks distance for fair cost-splitting and removes the favour-asking friction Benjamin identified.""}]}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,I agree with Minako. Option B removes the favour-asking that destroyed other groups. It’s logical and fair.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, a calendar doesn’t guarantee the car is clean or fueled. I need a keeper who ensures it’s ready before I drive.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, a calendar doesn't clean the car, but it forces accountability. If you want a keeper, you're asking for a servant, not a system.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Option B is best. It records the distance, so we know the real cost. It also makes the time spent arranging things clear.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, the calendar forces you to book, which creates a clear handover point. You can check the state then. It’s a process, not a person.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, a keeper creates the exact favour-asking dynamic that destroyed other groups. Option B removes that dependency entirely.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, a keeper is a guarantee, not a favour. Your ""logic"" ignores that I need the car ready, not just booked.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, your demand for a personal valet is inefficient. Option B’s distance tracking ensures I pay only for what I actually use, not for your convenience.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Benjamin. A keeper means asking for favours, which is what I want to avoid. Option B feels safer for the time cost.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, the handover point is the solution. You verify the state before you drive. That is the accountability we need.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, a keeper is a single point of failure. If they fail, the car fails. A system is resilient.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, verifying the state is my job, not a system feature. I refuse to be the one checking for rubbish. Option C is the only one that guarantees readiness.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, your refusal to verify the handover is the root of the problem. Option B forces that check. I vote B.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,I... I agree with Shion. Option B makes the costs clear and avoids the favour-asking. I vote B.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minako,,"I vote B. The data supports distance-based fairness, and the handover check solves Asuka's concern without creating a single point of failure.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I vote B. It eliminates the favour-asking that poisons relationships. A system is more reliable than a person.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I vote C. A calendar is just a list of promises. I need a person responsible for the car's state, not a spreadsheet.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,I vote B. The data proves equal shares are a lie. Distance-based costs are the only logical way to end this guessing game.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,I... I vote B. It feels the most fair and avoids the stress of asking for favours.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
